      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The prevalence of obesity is on the upward trend world-wide. This epidemic has challenging implications for anaesthetists, following the anthropometric changes associated with the disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To highlight some of the challenges, the management and the lessons learnt during the management of this patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This is a case report of a 52-year old super morbidly obese, diabetic, and hypertensive patient that presented for total abdominal hysterectomy. Surgery was carried out under a single-shot spinal anaesthesia with bupivacaine/fentanyl."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Under bupivacaine/fentanyl anaesthesia, she became very drowsy and had moderate to severe respiratory depression. She was arousable but had an obstructive sleep apnoea. Surgery was carried out successfully"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "A better understanding of the pathophysiology and complications that accompany obesity is needed to manage an obese patient under anaesthesia."
        }
      ]},
